"Multiplication For many applications, a fast estimation of the matrix product is good enough. In addition to hardware/software oriented opti- mizations such as cache management [ 18,23] or half-precision computations [ 40,55], MC methods [ 46] have been used for such188 estimations. At a high level, MC methods use repeated sampling and the law of large numbers to estimate aggregate values. Recall that given two matrices A∈Rm×n and B∈Rn×p, the product AB is an m×p matrix, where every element ABi,j is the inner product of i-th row of A with the j-th column of B: ABi,j = ⟨Ai,: , B:,j⟩ = ∑_{t=1}^n Ai,t Bt,j (5) In an MC estimation of ABi,j, instead of computing the sum over all t∈[1,n], only a small sample of elements Ω ⊂ {1,...,n}, where c=|Ω| < n, are considered. [15] introduce a nonuniform sampling method to generate Ω with a probability proportional to the magnitude of data. Specifically, they develop a randomized algorithm that samples each column i of A and row i of B with probability pi = ||A:,i|| · ||B:,i|| / ∑_{t=1}^n ||A:,t|| · ||B:,t||, (6) where ||·|| is the l2 norm. They define the matrices C ∈ Rm×c and R ∈ Rc×p as C = A S D and R = (S D)^T B, respectively. S is then defined as an n×c sampling matrix, with Sij = 1 if the i-th row of A is the j-th sample. D is a c×c diagonal scaling matrix with Djj = 1 / √(p_i). The authors prove that defining p_i as in Equation 6 minimizes the expected estimation error, E[ ||AB - C R||_F ], where ||·||_F is the Frobenius norm. Each element ABi,j is estimated as ∑_{t=1}^c C_{i,t} R_{t,j} = ∑_{t=1}^c (1 / √(p_i)) A_{i,t} B_{t,j} ≈ ABi,j. Sampling row-column pairs w.r.t p_i reduces the complexity of matrix multiplication to O(m c p)."

